glm's suite is labelled OVER-FITTED because at N=2 every failing suite is N-1-of-N. That label
is meaningless at this size, which is what `suite_verdict` was merged to say, and it is wrong
here: the two failures are real.

    an_error_printed_by_a_failing_test_does_not_void_the_names
      left:  NoCompile { why: Other, first_error: "error: expected 4 exits, got 3" }
      right: Fail { failed: ["error_scan"] }

    failed_run_with_unparseable_names_and_an_error_line_is_other
      left:  NoCompile { why: Environment, first_error: "(no output)" }
      right: NoCompile { why: Other, first_error: "error: test failed, to rerun pass ..." }

The second is a boundary I PINNED: a `Fail` with no parseable names returns `Other` carrying the
error line when one exists, and `Environment`/`(no output)` only when none does. gemini returns
the fallback while the line is right there. The first is the same hazard one level out -- a
failing test that PRINTS an error-shaped line. My spec pinned that a test NAME cannot trip the
scan and said nothing about a test's OUTPUT, and glm's reading is the one that keeps the names.

BOTH CRITIQUES FOUND A REAL DEFECT, FROM OPPOSITE SIDES OF THE SAME CONTRADICTION IN MY SPEC.

gemini on glm:

  "any unclassified compiler error ... occurring in a multi-crate invocation alongside an
   earlier crate's `test result: ok.` is completely swallowed and reported as `Cell::Pass`.
   This directly contradicts Clause 1 and the superset requirement that an unrecognised build
   failure is `Other` and never `Pass`."

glm on gemini:

  "The `read_cell` doc promises '`test result: ok.` and no failures -> `Pass`', but a bare
   error-initial line preempts ok ... Genuinely two-readable -- but their own doc contradicts
   their code, so one of the two is wrong regardless of which reading wins."

They are describing one hole from either end. Clause 1 says output containing `test result: ok.`
and no failures is `Pass`; the superset section says an unrecognised build failure is never
`Pass`. When both appear, my spec says both things. N-of-N, mine, filed.

glm was also scrupulous about its own advantage: it flagged that gemini's tests pin whitespace-
only output and a bare `Killed` sentinel, neither of which the spec fixes, and added "Both are
reasonable resolutions (I made the same ones)". A critic disclosing that it shares the flaw it
is reporting is the behaviour this stage exists to produce.

DECISION. glm on 21 tests to 17, on the only discriminating suite in the field, and on gemini
violating a boundary the spec pins by name. Its own defect -- swallowing an unclassified error
after an earlier crate's `ok.` -- is real, was found by gemini, and is filed rather than fixed
on the way in, because editing a winner's diff during a merge puts the adjudicator's preferences
into the record unmeasured.

